Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the numerator is $x^5$, let's divide by $x^5$.

In the denominator, let's divide by $-\sqrt{x^{10}}$, since for negative values, $x^5=-\sqrt{x^{10}}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{x^5+4x^2}{\sqrt{x^{10}+8x^7}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{x^5+4x^2}{x^5}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{x^{10}+8x^7}}{-\sqrt{x^{10}}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x^5=-\sqrt{x^{10}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\dfrac{x^5+4x^2}{x^5}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{x^{10}+8x^7}}{\sqrt{x^{10}}}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\dfrac{1\cancel{x^5}}{\cancel{x^5}}+\dfrac{4\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}\cdot x^3}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{1\cancel{x^{10}}}{\cancel{x^{10}}}+\dfrac{8\cancel{x^7}}{\cancel{x^7} \cdot x^3}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{1+\dfrac{4}{x^3}}{\sqrt{1+\dfrac{8}{x^3}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{1+0}{\sqrt{1+0}}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1}}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{1}{1}
\\\\
&=-1
\end{align}$
